= Self-organized criticality — Technical =

'''Self-organized criticality''' (SOC) is a framework for driven dissipative systems that approach scale-invariant critical-like dynamics through internal evolution rather than by externally tuning an ordinary control parameter to a critical value. Its model systems can be analyzed rigorously, but empirical SOC claims require more than observing heavy-tailed events.

== BTW sandpile ==

On a finite square lattice, let site <math>i</math> have integer height <math>z_i</math>. Slow drive adds one grain,

<math>z_i\leftarrow z_i+1.</math>

If

<math>z_i\ge z_c=4,</math>

the site topples,

<math>z_i\leftarrow z_i-4,</math>

and each nearest neighbor receives one grain. Grains leaving open boundaries are dissipated. The avalanche ends when all sites are stable.

Bak, Tang, and Wiesenfeld developed this canonical construction in 1987–1988.<ref name="btw">[https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.59.381 Bak, Tang and Wiesenfeld (1987), “Self-Organized Criticality: An Explanation of 1/f Noise.”]</ref>

== Abelian property ==

In the standard BTW model, the final stable configuration and number of topplings do not depend on the order in which unstable sites are relaxed, under the usual finite-lattice rules.

This Abelian property simplifies analysis.

It should not be assumed for arbitrary threshold systems.

== Drive and relaxation ==

Canonical SOC separates time scales:

<math>t_{drive}\gg t_{relax}.</math>

A new grain is added only after the preceding avalanche terminates.

Finite-rate drive can cause avalanches to overlap and can alter scaling.

The driving protocol is therefore part of the model.

== Stationarity ==

After a transient, mean input balances mean dissipation:

<math>\langle I\rangle=\langle D\rangle.</math>

Measurements intended to characterize the stationary state should exclude or separately analyze the transient.

A nonstationary trend can create apparent long-range correlations without criticality.

== Avalanche observables ==

For avalanche <math>a</math>, define quantities such as:

* size <math>s_a</math> — total topplings;
* area <math>A_a</math> — distinct sites toppled;
* duration <math>T_a</math>;
* spatial radius <math>R_a</math>.

These observables need not share the same exponent.

Update convention can especially affect duration.

== Finite-size scaling ==

A common scaling ansatz is

<math>P(s;L)=s^{-\tau_s}F(s/L^{D_s}),</math>

where <math>L</math> is system size and <math>F</math> gives the finite-size cutoff.

The characteristic cutoff scales as

<math>s_c(L)\sim L^{D_s}.</math>

Evidence for SOC is stronger when the cutoff grows systematically with <math>L</math> and rescaled distributions collapse.

== Data collapse ==

Under the scaling hypothesis, plotting

<math>s^{\tau_s}P(s;L)</math>

against

<math>s/L^{D_s}</math>

should approximately collapse different system sizes onto a common curve.

Visual collapse alone is insufficient; exponent uncertainty and quantitative goodness of collapse should be reported.

== Moment scaling ==

Moments

<math>\langle s^q\rangle=\int s^qP(s;L)\,ds</math>

can scale as

<math>\langle s^q\rangle\sim L^{\sigma(q)}.</math>

Moment analysis can estimate exponent combinations and identify departures from simple scaling.

== Power-law estimation ==

For continuous observations <math>s_i\ge s_{min}</math>, a simple maximum-likelihood estimator is

<math>\hat\alpha=1+n\left[\sum_i\ln(s_i/s_{min})\right]^{-1}.</math>

Discrete avalanche sizes require the discrete likelihood.

The lower cutoff should be estimated or justified rather than chosen for visual appearance.

== Alternative distributions ==

Compare the power-law model with plausible alternatives:

* lognormal;
* exponential;
* stretched exponential;
* truncated power law.

Likelihood-based comparisons and simulation-based goodness-of-fit are preferable to least-squares fitting on log-log axes.

Therefore:

<pre>
straight line on log-log axes
≠ proof of a power law
≠ proof of SOC
</pre>

== Correlations ==

A candidate critical system can also show long-range correlations.

Spatial correlation:

<math>C(r)=\langle x_i x_{i+r}\rangle-\langle x\rangle^2.</math>

At criticality, decay can be algebraic over a scaling range rather than characterized by one short correlation length.

Direct correlation measurements strengthen evidence beyond event histograms.

== Power spectra ==

The original SOC proposal associated critical dynamics with flicker-like spectra,

<math>S(f)\propto f^{-\beta}.</math>

Many non-SOC mechanisms also produce <math>1/f</math>-type noise.

A spectral exponent therefore does not identify the mechanism.

== Conservation and dissipation ==

In the BTW bulk, toppling is locally conservative.

Dissipation occurs at the open boundary.

Conservation, slow driving, and dissipation are important for many SOC universality classes.

Changing bulk conservation can change or eliminate the critical regime.

== Universality ==

Critical models can share exponents despite microscopic differences.

SOC models can belong to different universality classes depending on:

* conservation;
* stochasticity;
* symmetry;
* dimensionality.

The deterministic BTW and stochastic sandpile families need not have identical exponent structure.

== Branching-process approximation ==

Avalanche propagation can be approximated through branching ratio

<math>\sigma=E[number\ of\ descendants\ per\ active\ site].</math>

Subcritical systems have <math>\sigma<1</math>, critical systems <math>\sigma=1</math>, and supercritical systems <math>\sigma>1</math> in the simplified branching framework.

This is an analogy and reduced model, not proof that the original system is a branching process.

== Real granular systems ==

Real sand and rice involve:

* friction;
* inertia;
* particle shape;
* packing;
* rolling.

Frette et al. found scale-free-like avalanche behavior for elongated rice grains but not for less elongated grains, demonstrating that granular behavior depends on dissipation mechanism and particle properties.<ref name="rice">[https://doi.org/10.1038/379049a0 Frette, V. et al. (1996), “Avalanche dynamics in a pile of rice,” ''Nature'' 379, 49–52.]</ref>

== Finite-scale false positives ==

Vieira developed models that show nearly SOC-like behavior at small sizes while large characteristic avalanches dominate as system size increases.<ref name="vieira">[https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevE.66.051306 Vieira, M. de S. (2002), “Breakdown of self-organized criticality in sandpiles.”]</ref>

This shows why one finite system size can give misleading evidence.

Asymptotic behavior matters.

== Measurement window ==

Empirical systems are observed for finite time.

Rare large events may be absent from a short sample.

Tail exponents can drift as additional data accumulate.

Uncertainty should include temporal sampling.

== Avalanche detection threshold ==

Continuous observations are often converted into events using thresholds or temporal bins.

Changing the threshold can merge or split avalanches.

Sensitivity analysis should therefore report how the inferred scaling changes with event-definition parameters.

== Neural avalanches ==

Neural criticality studies often define avalanches from population activity.

Results depend on:

* electrode coverage;
* threshold;
* bin width;
* sampling rate;
* subsampling.

Critical neural dynamics and self-organized criticality are not synonyms.

A system can be maintained near a critical point by explicit regulatory mechanisms rather than BTW-style self-organization.

== Subsampling ==

If the full system contains <math>N</math> units but only <math>n\ll N</math> are observed, event distributions can be distorted.

A measurement model should simulate the effect of subsampling before interpreting exponents.

== Earthquake applications ==

Earthquake catalogs show scale-free features such as magnitude-frequency relations.

SOC models can reproduce avalanche-like stress release.

However, tectonic loading, friction, spatial heterogeneity, and fault mechanics must be modeled explicitly.

One power-law statistic cannot identify SOC.

== Solar-flare applications ==

Solar-flare energies have motivated avalanche models.

A defensible model should predict more than the event-energy histogram, such as:

* waiting-time behavior;
* spatial structure;
* correlations;
* system-size effects.

Otherwise many mechanisms remain compatible with the data.

== Self-tuning versus SOC ==

A system can remain near criticality through explicit feedback.

For example,

<math>\dot\theta=-k(O-O^*)</math>

regulates control parameter <math>\theta</math> toward a target observable <math>O^*</math>.

This is self-tuning criticality but not necessarily canonical SOC.

Mechanisms should be named precisely.

== Absorbing-state perspective ==

Some SOC models can be related to an underlying absorbing-state phase transition.

Slow drive and dissipation can cause the driven system to remain near the critical point of a corresponding fixed-energy model.

This provides a more formal route to self-tuning in specific model families.

The mapping is not universal.

== Simulation protocol ==

A reproducible BTW simulation should report:

<pre>
initialize stable lattice
burn in for specified number of grain additions
for each measured addition:
    add one grain
    relax completely
    record size, area, duration
repeat across several lattice sizes and seeds
</pre>

Record boundary conditions and toppling schedule.

== Efficient implementation ==

A queue of unstable sites avoids rescanning the full lattice after each toppling.

Runtime then scales more closely with avalanche activity.

Implementation optimization should preserve the exact model dynamics.

== Synchronous versus sequential updates ==

The BTW Abelian property preserves stable outcome under legal toppling order, but avalanche duration can differ depending on whether topplings are grouped in parallel waves or executed sequentially.

Therefore temporal observables require a stated update convention.

== Dependence between avalanches ==

Successive avalanches can be correlated.

Treating all events as IID can underestimate statistical uncertainty.

Block bootstrap or correlation-aware methods can be appropriate.

== Falsification criteria ==

Evidence against a proposed SOC mechanism can include:

* a characteristic event scale that does not grow with system size;
* failure of finite-size collapse;
* nonstationarity;
* required fine tuning;
* better-supported alternative dynamics.

A scientific SOC claim should specify what result would count against it.

== SOC versus self-organization ==

[[Self-organization]] is broader.

A Turing pattern or synchronized oscillator can self-organize without criticality.

SOC adds the claim of critical-like scale invariance and a self-organizing route toward it.

== SOC versus emergence ==

[[Emergence]] is broader still.

Emergent macrobehavior does not imply criticality.

The terms should never be treated as interchangeable labels for complexity.

== Cross-domain checklist ==

Before applying SOC to a new domain, identify:

# drive;
# dissipation;
# local threshold or instability mechanism;
# time-scale structure;
# stationary regime;
# system-size scaling;
# alternative models.

If these cannot be specified, the use of SOC may be metaphorical rather than mechanistic.

== Epistemic boundary ==

SOC is a well-developed model framework.

Whether a particular empirical system is self-organized critical is a separate hypothesis.

No single signature—power law, avalanche, fractal structure, or <math>1/f</math> spectrum—is sufficient by itself.

